WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:07.759
If you’ve ever been to the sea, you might
have noticed there’s an awful lot of it.

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:12.440
Oceans cover more than seventy per cent of
the earth’s surface and impact your daily

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.390
life in ways that probably never even occur
to you.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:21.699
They regulate the global climate and drive
weather systems, so no matter how far you

00:00:21.699 --> 00:00:27.320
live from the coast, oceans had some influence
on what you chose to wear today.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.640
Nice mankini, by the way.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.340
And look around you - no, not literally, keep
watching me - your life as you know it would

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.000
be quite different without marine transport.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:44.210
About ninety per cent of the stuff that’s
traded around the world is carried by ships,

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:50.350
which includes everything from food to clothes
to furniture and cars, even the fuel in those

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:54.620
cars and the smelly things you hang from the
rearview mirror.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:59.010
If it weren’t for sea travel, you probably
wouldn’t have the device you’re watching

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:01.330
this video on right now.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:06.899
You probably wouldn’t even be alive, since
oceans are widely regarded as the birthplace

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:08.280
of life itself.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:12.409
So, yeah, they’re a pretty big deal.

00:01:12.409 --> 00:01:18.679
You would think we’d know more about something
so important to us, but it seems we’re still

00:01:18.679 --> 00:01:21.170
mostly clueless about the sea.

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:26.060
More than eighty percent of the world’s
ocean depths are still unexplored.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:29.549
Like oysters, they remain a big, wet, salty
mystery.

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:33.710
But the riddles don’t just lie beneath the
waves.

00:01:33.710 --> 00:01:39.280
For as long as humans have been sailing the
high seas, they’ve been collecting tales

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:47.909
about strange phenomena like mermaids, sirens,
sea monsters, magical realms, and haunted

00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:48.909
ships.

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:54.460
Of course, most of these stories come from
sex-starved men full of gonorrhoea and rum,

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.470
so they should probably be taken with a pinch
of sea salt.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:06.350
There is one genuine mystery, though, that
remains unsolved almost 150 years after it

00:02:06.350 --> 00:03:19.689
was discovered: the bizarre account of the
ghost ship, Mary Celeste.

00:03:19.689 --> 00:03:37.219
It 

00:03:37.219 --> 00:03:40.659
was the fourth of December 1872.

00:03:40.659 --> 00:03:46.799
In the preceding months, Mount Vesuvius had
erupted in Italy, the first-ever FA Cup Final

00:03:46.799 --> 00:03:51.780
had been held in England, and some guy had
patented dried milk.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:57.409
A week, later a meteorite would strike the
earth near a small town called Banbury.

00:03:57.409 --> 00:04:03.680
But none of this mattered to Captain David
Morehouse, commander of the Canadian ship

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:10.150
Dei Gratia, as he stared through his telescope
at a vessel on the horizon drifting aimlessly

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:12.590
in the north-Atlantic Ocean.

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:14.349
Morehouse knew the boat.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:20.400
It was the Mary Celeste, a cargo ship that
had left New York eight days before him, loaded

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:26.050
with about 1,700 barrels of denatured alcohol.

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:28.300
Denatured alcohol isn’t like rum.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:34.660
It has an alcohol content between 70 and 99
per cent and is used as a solvent, cleaning

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.850
agent, disinfectant, and fuel.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:41.200
Drinking it will make you blind or dead.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:43.770
Okay, maybe it is a bit like rum.

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:49.680
By that stage in her career, the Mary Celeste
had already accumulated a fair bit of bad

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:50.680
fortune.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.139
Originally registered under the name ‘the
Amazon’, her maiden voyage in 1861 from

00:04:55.139 --> 00:05:03.780
Nova Scotia to London was delayed when, just
before her departure, the captain fell ill

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:04.780
and died.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:11.990
Then, with a new captain, the Amazon sailed
across the Atlantic but collided with fishing

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.252
equipment off the coast of Eastport, Maine,
before running into and sinking another ship

00:05:16.252 --> 00:05:18.169
in the English Channel.

00:05:18.169 --> 00:05:27.270
A few uneventful years passed until October
1867 when the Amazon ran ashore during a storm,

00:05:27.270 --> 00:05:31.830
being so badly damaged that her owners abandoned
her as a wreck.

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:37.990
The derelict boat was eventually bought and
sold a number of times before it was restored

00:05:37.990 --> 00:05:42.130
and renamed the Mary Celeste.

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:47.270
When Captain Morehouse found the ship floating
in the open sea there’s a good chance he

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:52.470
knew nothing of her chequered past, but if
he had, it’s safe to say alarm bells would

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:58.819
have been rigging as he inspected the ship
more closely, noticing she was moving erratically

00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:01.960
and that her sails looked odd.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:07.220
He also knew the Mary Celeste should have
already arrived in Genoa, Italy, so, realising

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:12.790
something wasn’t right, the captain changed
course to investigate.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:18.960
When members of the Dei Gratia crew boarded
the Mary Celeste they found her deserted,

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:25.020
but with no indications of where everyone
had gone or, more importantly, why.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:30.380
There were no signs of fire, violence or a
struggle.

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:36.289
The sails were in bad shape, some missing
altogether, and the rigging - that’s what

00:06:36.289 --> 00:06:40.550
landlubbers like us call the system of ropes
which hold a sailing ship’s masts in place

00:06:40.550 --> 00:06:44.099
- was damaged and hanging out of place.

00:06:44.099 --> 00:06:48.319
The main hatch, which is the big hole in the
middle of the deck where you load and unload

00:06:48.319 --> 00:06:52.650
cargo, still had its cover on, but the covers
of the hatches at the front and back of the

00:06:52.650 --> 00:07:00.430
boat - fore and aft in sailing terms - were
off and lying beside the hatches on the deck.

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:07.169
The cargo of industrial alcohol was untouched,
which ruled out pirates or blind alcoholics

00:07:07.169 --> 00:07:09.490
as an obvious cause.

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:14.410
About three and a half feet of water sloshed
around the cargo hold, which was a significant

00:07:14.410 --> 00:07:17.159
amount but not alarming.

00:07:17.159 --> 00:07:23.080
Someone had disassembled one of the two pumps,
but the vessel was still perfectly seaworthy.

00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:26.509
The only thing of importance missing was the
lifeboat.

00:07:26.509 --> 00:07:30.159
Well, that and all the people, obviously.

00:07:30.159 --> 00:07:34.729
The crew’s belongings were still in their
quarters, and there was enough food and water

00:07:34.729 --> 00:07:37.900
on board to last another six months.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:42.770
Most of the ship's papers were missing along
with the captain's navigational instruments,

00:07:42.770 --> 00:07:50.000
and in the mate's cabin was the ship’s logbook
with its final entry dated at 8 a.m. on November

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:52.409
25, ten days earlier.

00:07:52.409 --> 00:07:58.240
The final logbook entry placed the Mary Celeste
within sight of Santa Maria Island in the

00:07:58.240 --> 00:08:05.180
Azores, a group of nine small volcanic islands
in the middle of nautical nowhere.

00:08:05.180 --> 00:08:11.569
By the time Morehouse and his crew discovered
her, she had drifted almost 750 kilometres

00:08:11.569 --> 00:08:13.439
to the east.

00:08:13.439 --> 00:08:19.289
Ten people were missing from the Mary Celeste,
including Captain Benjamin Spooner Briggs,

00:08:19.289 --> 00:08:25.240
his wife, Sarah, the couple's 2-year-old daughter,
Sophia, and seven crewmen.

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:29.850
None of them was ever seen or heard from again.

00:08:29.850 --> 00:08:34.300
Briggs was a part-owner of the boat and an
extremely experienced skipper.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:39.460
He handpicked his crew, and they were all
regarded as first-class sailors.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:45.990
Overall, a crack team and not the type to
abandon ship without a serious reason.

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:48.950
So, what was that reason?

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:56.850
Why and how did ten people disappear from
the Mary Celeste without a trace?

00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:02.220
These questions would soon fill newspapers
and books across the globe, but first, they

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:05.260
appeared in the law courts of Gibraltar.

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:11.029
After finding the Mary Celeste, Captain Morehouse
decided to sail her to Gibraltar, about 1,100

00:09:11.029 --> 00:09:14.400
kilometres away, so he could claim salvage.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:20.450
The law of salvage is an ancient part of international
maritime law that says anyone who helps rescue

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:27.610
or recover a ship or cargo is owed a reward
proportional to its value by the ship’s

00:09:27.610 --> 00:09:28.610
owners.

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:33.410
In this case, Morehouse and the crew of the
Dei Gratia had rescued the Mary Celeste and

00:09:33.410 --> 00:09:36.870
sailed her back to safety at their own risk.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:41.230
When the ship arrived in Gibraltar, she was
confiscated by the authorities until the salvage

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:44.040
hearings could be resolved in court.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.500
As part of those court proceedings, it was
suggested the disappearances of Captain Briggs,

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:54.450
his family and his crew, were down to foul
play.

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:59.020
Marks were found on the bow of the ship that
some claimed were made by an axe.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:05.090
There were also dark stains found both on
the boat and on the captain’s sword that

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:06.690
might have been blood.

00:10:06.690 --> 00:10:11.000
The attorney general in Gibraltar believed
the crew got drunk one night on the alcohol

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:15.680
in the ship’s hold - even though it was
undrinkable - and slaughtered Captain Briggs

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.950
and his family in a drunken frenzy before
escaping in the lifeboat.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:22.880
Just a regular Friday night, then.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.780
But, soon the results of scientific tests
proved nothing dodgy had happened aboard the

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:33.540
Mary Celeste and, after three months, the
authorities released the ship from custody.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:38.600
Captain Morehouse and his crew received less
than 8,000 dollars for their salvage efforts,

00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:43.209
which was well below the expected payment
given the value of the ship and cargo.

00:10:43.209 --> 00:10:48.920
Unfortunately for Morehouse, the court case
had generated rumours he and his men had killed

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:53.430
the occupants of the Mary Celeste so they
could claim the ship for salvage.

00:10:53.430 --> 00:11:00.139
That accusation wasn’t ever proved but the
doubt never quite faded, and by awarding such

00:11:00.139 --> 00:11:05.279
a small salvage settlement, it seemed the
court remained suspicious.

00:11:05.279 --> 00:11:11.230
Other theories of foul play have Briggs and
Morehouse as friends, conspiring to claim

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:16.340
the insurance money for the ship and staging
a disappearance.

00:11:16.340 --> 00:11:21.160
Another (admittedly somewhat ridiculous) view
was that Captain Briggs, who was a devout

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:26.339
Christian, had lost his mind in a religious
frenzy and murdered everyone on board before

00:11:26.339 --> 00:11:28.410
killing himself.

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:30.350
Just a regular Sunday, then.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:35.920
In the decades that followed, the story of
the Mary Celeste was retold and exaggerated

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:37.810
many times over.

00:11:37.810 --> 00:11:42.740
In 1883, The Los Angeles Times published a
false narrative in which the ship was found

00:11:42.740 --> 00:11:48.899
in perfect condition and fully rigged, the
cooking fire was burning in the galley stove,

00:11:48.899 --> 00:11:54.079
dinner was on the table, barely cold, and
the logbook was updated to the hour before

00:11:54.079 --> 00:11:56.030
the ship was found.

00:11:56.030 --> 00:12:00.810
The next year, Arthur Conan Doyle, the man
who would later become the author of Sherlock

00:12:00.810 --> 00:12:04.540
Holmes, wrote a short story based on the Mary
Celeste.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:10.029
It was a fictional piece but, as with the
LA Times story, much of it became accepted

00:12:10.029 --> 00:12:11.480
as fact.

00:12:11.480 --> 00:12:16.529
These kinds of fabrications have made it even
harder to come up with plausible explanations

00:12:16.529 --> 00:12:21.779
for what happened aboard Captain Briggs’
ship, but a few proposals have gained popularity

00:12:21.779 --> 00:12:22.779
over the years.

00:12:22.779 --> 00:12:28.449
Let’s get the first and most obvious one
out of the way: Briggs, his family and his

00:12:28.449 --> 00:12:32.120
crew were whisked away by aliens.

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:37.920
Since aliens are apparently responsible for
building the pyramids, making crop circles,

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:44.610
assassinating JFK, and impregnating my neighbour
Janet, I suppose we should at least consider

00:12:44.610 --> 00:12:49.370
the possibility they swooped down on a random
ship in the middle of the sea and kidnapped

00:12:49.370 --> 00:12:51.279
all those on board.

00:12:51.279 --> 00:12:54.740
Because aliens obviously have nothing better
to do with their time.

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:59.839
Another guess is that the Mary Celeste was
hit by a water spout, the oceanic equivalent

00:12:59.839 --> 00:13:04.180
of a tornado, which would explain all the
water in the ship’s hold.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:08.779
A water spout would also have caused the atmospheric
temperature to drop, which could have driven

00:13:08.779 --> 00:13:13.440
water from the bilges, the bottom part of
the ship where all the gunk collects, up into

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:14.750
the pumps.

00:13:14.750 --> 00:13:20.490
This might have convinced the crew the vessel
had taken on more water than she had, and

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:23.070
was therefore in danger of sinking.

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:29.339
Under those circumstances, Briggs might have
chosen to abandon ship while he was in sight

00:13:29.339 --> 00:13:30.980
of land.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:35.310
Building on the theme of natural disasters,
other commentators have theorised that an

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:41.160
earthquake on the seabed - imaginatively known
as a seaquake - may have generated enough

00:13:41.160 --> 00:13:45.980
turbulence aboard the Mary Celeste to cause
the spillage of alcohol and the release of

00:13:45.980 --> 00:13:47.120
alcohol fumes.

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:52.001
That would potentially explain the hatches,
which might have been opened in an attempt

00:13:52.001 --> 00:13:54.070
to air the cargo hold.

00:13:54.070 --> 00:13:58.790
It would also tie in with the nine empty barrels
that were found onboard.

00:13:58.790 --> 00:14:04.050
If there had been a build-up of fumes, Briggs
would have been concerned, and that concern

00:14:04.050 --> 00:14:09.791
might have been elevated by rumblings from
the cargo hold, the smell of fumes, perhaps

00:14:09.791 --> 00:14:10.990
even an explosion.

00:14:10.990 --> 00:14:16.300
In that case, Briggs and his crew might have
climbed into the lifeboat, attached it to

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:20.070
the ship, and allowed themselves to float
behind at a distance.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:26.690
That seems like a reasonable enough response
to the situation, but one has to question

00:14:26.690 --> 00:14:32.880
the sense in tying yourself to something you
think might be about to explode, especially

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:38.899
since a lifeboat in the open ocean is far
from a safe place to be.

00:14:38.899 --> 00:14:43.500
Next time you go swimming, throw a cork into
the pool and then do your best cannonball

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:49.250
- that’ll give you some idea of what a small
lifeboat might face in the open ocean.

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:55.040
All it would have taken was for the tow line
to come loose or snap and the ship’s occupants

00:14:55.040 --> 00:14:57.370
would have been in serious trouble.

00:14:57.370 --> 00:15:01.839
Some experts suggest the Mary Celeste would
have been a safer bet than the lifeboat even

00:15:01.839 --> 00:15:06.490
if she had suffered an explosion and that
Briggs would have been a fool to abandon ship.

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:11.640
But, the alcohol explosion view of events
had a lot of support at the time and gained

00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:19.300
some credibility from later reports of ship’s
whose cargo had exploded under similar conditions.

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:25.509
It was also noted the nine empty barrels were
made of red oak, unlike the other barrels

00:15:25.509 --> 00:15:27.820
which were made of white oak.

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:33.290
Red oak is more porous than white oak, and
it’s possible some of the alcohol may have

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:38.370
seeped out the barrels, filling the cargo
hold with more toxic fumes than the last time

00:15:38.370 --> 00:15:40.730
you farted under the duvet.

00:15:40.730 --> 00:15:45.240
The main argument against the idea the Mary
Celeste’s crew were trying to escape an

00:15:45.240 --> 00:15:51.779
explosion was that no scorch marks or evidence
of an explosion were ever found.

00:15:51.779 --> 00:15:59.440
But in 2006, an experiment carried out by
Dr Andrea Sella of University College London

00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:03.209
showed that such an outcome was perfectly
possible.

00:16:03.209 --> 00:16:08.209
Sella built a replica of the Mary Celeste’s
hold with paper cubes to represent barrels

00:16:08.209 --> 00:16:12.810
and then used butane to generate the type
of explosion that would have happened had

00:16:12.810 --> 00:16:15.870
alcohol fumes in the hold ignited.

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:22.399
The results showed a massive explosion with
no burning, no scorching, and no soot left

00:16:22.399 --> 00:16:23.399
behind.

00:16:23.399 --> 00:16:28.720
According to Sella, the trapped gas created
a pressure-wave type of explosion with a spectacular

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:32.470
surge of flame followed by relatively cool
air.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:36.779
Such an eruption would have been enough to
blow open the hatches and have everyone fleeing

00:16:36.779 --> 00:16:41.430
the ship like, well, rats fleeing a sinking
ship.

00:16:41.430 --> 00:16:47.079
To many, an alcohol-fuelled explosion and
a desperate evacuation is the most logical

00:16:47.079 --> 00:16:52.160
and scientifically sound explanation of what
happened to those aboard the Mary Celeste.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:58.120
But forensic researcher, Anne McGregor, thinks
there’s more to the story.

00:16:58.120 --> 00:17:03.490
After cross-referencing notes from the salvage
trial with the ship’s log and oceanic data

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:08.950
from the time, MacGregor concluded Briggs’
instruments were probably damaged because

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:15.070
his measurements appeared to be wrong - he
was actually 120 nautical miles west of where

00:17:15.070 --> 00:17:16.710
he thought he was.

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:21.850
It seems the ship had experienced lousy weather
the night before the final logbook recording,

00:17:21.850 --> 00:17:26.970
and with the pumps broken - perhaps due to
coal dust buildup from a previous cargo - the

00:17:26.970 --> 00:17:31.840
crew had no way to measure how much water
had been taken on in the rough seas.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:36.430
If Briggs suspected there was too much water
on board, he would have had a decision to

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:37.540
make.

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:42.850
If he pressed on, he knew they wouldn’t
see land for hundreds of miles, so if there

00:17:42.850 --> 00:17:48.600
was a possibility the ship was sinking, their
best chance would be to head for Santa Maria

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:54.610
island - which by his faulty calculations
was nearby - while they still could.

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:59.720
That might not have been the best decision,
but it wouldn’t have been crazy.

00:17:59.720 --> 00:18:04.390
One issue with many of these theories is that
Captain Briggs not only abandoned his ship,

00:18:04.390 --> 00:18:06.910
he also abandoned his log book.

00:18:06.910 --> 00:18:11.290
If he really did think the ship was slowly
sinking, he would still have had plenty of

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:15.970
time to collect the log, or at least to scribble
a quick note as to his plans.

00:18:15.970 --> 00:18:19.220
Ships’ captains in the nineteenth century
were not in the business of forgetting to

00:18:19.220 --> 00:18:23.960
make crucial entries in their logbooks, no
matter the circumstances.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:28.820
Of course, we’ll never know for sure what
happened to those on board the Mary Celeste,

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:33.430
but we do know the ship’s bad run continued
long after the mysterious disappearance of

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:34.840
her crew.

00:18:34.840 --> 00:18:39.460
When the trial in Gibraltar was concluded
she sailed to Italy, as originally planned,

00:18:39.460 --> 00:18:42.710
to deliver her cargo, before heading to New
York.

00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:49.130
But by the time the Mary Celeste arrived in
the Big Apple, the rumours of bloodshed, murder,

00:18:49.130 --> 00:18:54.650
mutinee and inexplicable disappearances had
circulated throughout the shipping world and

00:18:54.650 --> 00:18:57.370
nobody wanted anything to do with her.

00:18:57.370 --> 00:19:00.910
Eventually the owners were forced to sell
her at a considerable loss.

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:06.080
The new owners didn’t fare much better,
with the ship consistently losing money, and

00:19:06.080 --> 00:19:12.260
in 1879 she called in at St Helena bay to
seek medical help when the captain fell sick.

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:18.060
He died on the island shortly after in a sequence
of events eerily similar to the way in which

00:19:18.060 --> 00:19:21.230
the ship’s first captain had died.

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:28.940
All told, the Mary Celeste had seen three
captains die prematurely, fuelling speculations

00:19:28.940 --> 00:19:31.920
the ship was cursed.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:38.420
Six years later luck still hadn’t improved
and the desperate skipper, a Captain Parker,

00:19:38.420 --> 00:19:43.290
conspired with a group of shippers in a crooked
plot to finally make some cash on what had

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.590
been a truly terrible investment.

00:19:45.590 --> 00:19:51.360
They filled the Mary Celeste with worthless
cargo, which they insured for 30,000 dollars

00:19:51.360 --> 00:19:57.590
- about 850,000 dollars in today’s money
- before deliberately running the vessel aground

00:19:57.590 --> 00:20:00.650
near Haiti, wrecking her forever.

00:20:00.650 --> 00:20:06.300
Investigations by the insurance company revealed
the attempted trickery, and Captain Parker

00:20:06.300 --> 00:20:12.070
and his accomplices were soon facing trial
in Boston for conspiracy to commit insurance

00:20:12.070 --> 00:20:13.070
fraud.

00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:18.040
Parker was also charged with "wilfully casting
away a ship," a crime known as barratry that

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:20.890
used to be punishable by death.

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:24.950
Luckily for the captain he avoided the death
penalty and the jury was split on the crime

00:20:24.950 --> 00:20:31.810
of fraud, so Parker went free, but his reputation
was ruined and he died in poverty just three

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:33.210
months later.

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:38.020
One of his fellow defendants went mad and
another killed himself.

00:20:38.020 --> 00:20:42.510
Even under normal circumstances this would
be an almost unbelievable chain of events,

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:47.840
but looking back it seems very little was
normal about the Mary Celeste, or those who

00:20:47.840 --> 00:20:50.270
came in contact with her.

00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:55.710
Perhaps it's for the best she now lies in
a permanent, watery grave at the bottom of

00:20:55.710 --> 00:20:59.120
the ocean.

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:04.630
Thanks for watching.

